window.docContents[50].resolve({"/Statistical-Risk-and-Markov-Kernels/#Statlib--Statistical-Risk-and-Markov-Kernels--Measure-indexed-statistical-risk":{"contents":"In many applications, one works at a lower level of generality with a class of\nprobability measures on \\Omega_n. The measure-indexed setup consists of:\n\n* a sequence indexed by n \\in \\mathbb{N};* a measurable space (\\Omega_n,\\mathcal{G}_n) for experimental randomness;* a class of probability measures \\mathcal{P}_n on\n  (\\Omega_n,\\mathcal{G}_n);* a measurable space (S_n,\\mathcal{F}_n) for the object of interest and a\n  measurable functional \\Psi_n : \\mathcal{P}_n \\to S_n;* a measurable observation space (\\mathcal{X}_n,\\mathcal{B}_n) and a\n  measurable observation map X_n : \\Omega_n \\to \\mathcal{X}_n;* a measurable decision space (\\mathcal{Y}_n,\\mathcal{C}_n) and a\n  randomized decision rule from (\\mathcal{X}_n,\\mathcal{B}_n) to\n  (\\mathcal{Y}_n,\\mathcal{C}_n);* a jointly measurable loss function\n  \\ell_n : \\mathcal{Y}_n \\times S_n \\to [0,+\\infty].\n\nThe average loss at P_n \\in \\mathcal{P}_n is\n\n\\operatorname{Risk}(T_n,\\Psi_n;P_n)\n   =\n   \\int\\!\\int\n     \\ell_n(y_n,\\Psi_n(P_n))\\,\n     dT_n(X_n(\\omega),dy_n)\\,\n     dP_n(\\omega).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Statlib\u0009Statistical Risk and Markov Kernels","header":"2.3. Measure-indexed statistical risk","id":"/Statistical-Risk-and-Markov-Kernels/#Statlib--Statistical-Risk-and-Markov-Kernels--Measure-indexed-statistical-risk"}});